  "actionExerciseGuide": "Form tips",
  "captionExerciseGuide": "General tips for this movement pattern — not specific instructions for this exact exercise.",
  "exerciseGuideVerticalPush": "Press straight up and slightly back, finishing over your head rather than out in front. Keep your ribs down and core braced — don't arch your lower back to force out extra range.",
  "exerciseGuideHorizontalPush": "Drive the weight away from your chest in a straight line, elbows at roughly 45° to your torso. Keep your shoulder blades pulled together and down — flared elbows or rolled-forward shoulders are what strain the shoulder here.",
  "exerciseGuideVerticalPull": "Pull your elbows down and back toward your hips, rather than just bending your arms. Lead with your chest and keep your shoulders away from your ears — don't swing or yank the weight down with momentum.",
  "exerciseGuideHorizontalPull": "Pull your elbows straight back past your ribs and squeeze your shoulder blades together at the end. Keep your torso still — the pull should come from your back, not a jerk from your lower back.",
  "exerciseGuideSquat": "Push your hips back and bend your knees together, chest up, weight through the middle of your feet. Track your knees over your toes instead of letting them cave in, and only go as deep as you can without your lower back rounding.",
  "exerciseGuideHinge": "Push your hips back while keeping the bar close to your legs and your spine neutral, then drive your hips forward to stand up. This is a hip movement, not a squat or a back lift — a rounding lower back is the main thing to watch.",
  "exerciseGuideCurl": "Keep your elbows pinned to your sides and move at a controlled tempo, especially on the way down. If you need to swing your back or shoulders to lift it, the weight is too heavy.",
  "exerciseGuideExtension": "Isolate the movement to just the joint involved and keep the rest of your body still. Control the weight back to the start instead of letting it drop — the lowering half does real work too.",
  "exerciseGuideCore": "Move with control and breathe steadily instead of holding your breath through the hard part. Clean form matters more than speed or rushing out extra reps.",
